# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
kant-practical-1.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: kant-practical-1.logic.r1 has "touchstone" and "touchstone → apriori_only" and "touchstone → ¬hume_skeptic" and "exp_judgements"; kant-practical-1.logic.r1.2 and kant-practical-1.logic.r1.3 have "touchstone" and "touchstone → apriori_only" and "exp_judgements"; they differ when only "absolute scepticism is rightly attributed to Hume in an unqualified sense", "Hume left at least one certain touchstone", "such a touchstone can only be found in a priori principles" and "experience consists not only of feelings but also of judgements" are true, which the second allows
kant-practical-1.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps kant-practical-1.logic.r1, to: Suppose "absolute scepticism is rightly attributed to Hume in an unqualified sense", "Hume left at least one certain touchstone", "such a touchstone can only be found in a priori principles" and "experience consists not only of feelings but also of judgements" are true. Is S4, "It is erroneous to attribute this in such an unqualified sense to Hume,  since he left at least one certain touchstone (which can only be found in a priori principles), although experience consists not only of feelings, but also of judgements.", true then?
kant-practical-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: kant-practical-1.logic.r1 has "driven_doubt → skeptic"; kant-practical-1.logic.r1.2 has "clash ∧ driven_doubt ∧ emp_felt → skeptic" and "touchstone → ¬hume_skeptic"; they differ when only "the greatest possible evidence of demonstration is in manifest contradiction with the alleged conclusions from experience", "we are driven to ask, like Cheselden's blind patient, which deceives me, sight or touch", "empiricism is based on a necessity felt", "universal empiricism reveals itself as absolute scepticism", "absolute scepticism is rightly attributed to Hume in an unqualified sense" and "Hume left at least one certain touchstone" are true, which the first allows; kant-practical-1.logic.r1.3 has "clash ∧ driven_doubt → skeptic" and "touchstone → ¬hume_skeptic"
kant-practical-1.md:1: argue/negation: kant-practical-1.logic.r1 P3 (S1): the sentence says "not", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if this science (mathematics) is in contradiction with a reason that admits only empirical principles, then (the greatest possible evidence of demonstration is in manifest contradiction with the alleged conclusions from experience and we are driven to ask, like Cheselden's blind patient, which deceives me, sight or touch)"
kant-practical-1.md:1: argue/negation: kant-practical-1.logic.r1.2 P3 (S1): the sentence says "not", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if this science (mathematics) is in contradiction with a reason that admits only empirical principles, then (the greatest possible evidence of demonstration is in manifest contradiction with the alleged conclusions from experience and we are driven to ask, like Cheselden's blind patient, which deceives me, sight or touch)"
kant-practical-1.md:1: argue/negation: kant-practical-1.logic.r1.3 P3 (S1): the sentence says "not", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if this science (mathematics) is in contradiction with a reason that admits only empirical principles, then (the greatest possible evidence of demonstration is in manifest contradiction with the alleged conclusions from experience and we are driven to ask, like Cheselden's blind patient, which deceives me, sight or touch)"
